  eyebrow="Where to go"
  readTime="9 min read"
>
  <p>
    New South Wales has more verified, bookable overnight walking tracks than
    most beginners realise, from a two-day coastal walk south of Sydney to a
    five-day hut-to-hut traverse on the mid-north coast. The hard part is not
    finding a track. It is working out which one suits your fitness and gear,
    and getting the booking and fire-season rules right before you leave.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide sticks to routes we could verify against NSW National Parks and
    Wildlife Service (NPWS) and NSW Rural Fire Service (RFS) sources, with the
    source linked next to each claim. If a distance, fee or booking rule could
    not be confirmed, it is left out rather than guessed.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        NSW has verified multi-day routes from a 27km two-day coastal walk to a
        260km long-distance track you can section-hike over multiple trips.
      </li>
      <li>
        Almost every NPWS campsite, including free ones, needs an online booking
        before you arrive.
      </li>
      <li>
        For remote or unbooked backcountry camping, NPWS asks you to lodge a
        free Trip Intention Form so search and rescue know your plan.
      </li>
      <li>
        The NSW statewide Bush Fire Danger Period runs 1 October to 31 March,
        and fuel stove rules change on Total Fire Ban days.
      </li>
      <li>
        Blue Mountains routes are covered separately in our <a
          href="/guides/overnight-hikes-blue-mountains/"
          >overnight hikes Blue Mountains guide</a
        >; this guide covers the rest of the state.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your <a href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/"
          >overnight hike packing list</a
        > and sleep system matter as much as the track you pick.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

