  <h2>What are the best overnight hikes in NSW for beginners?</h2>
  <p>
    Among the best overnight hikes in NSW, the Coast Track is a simpler trip to
    organise: two days, one campsite, and Sydney rail access at both ends. NPWS
    grades its 27 km route as Grade 4, so prepare for a demanding walk with a
    full pack, enough water and suitable weather. Yuraygir and Green Gully suit
    hikers who already have one overnight trip behind them and want a longer,
    more remote route. The Main Range walk and Great North Walk sit at the two
    extremes: a short alpine overnight near Thredbo, and a long-distance track
    most people section-hike over several trips rather than walking end to end.
  </p>

  <h2>What about overnight hikes in the Blue Mountains?</h2>
  <p>
    The Blue Mountains has its own dense network of NPWS overnight tracks and
    campsites within day-trip range of Sydney, so it gets its own dedicated
    guide rather than a summary here. If Katoomba, the Grose Valley or the Six
    Foot Track area is your target, start with our <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hikes-blue-mountains/"
      >overnight hikes Blue Mountains guide</a
    > for verified routes, campsites and bookings specific to that region.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you book campsites for overnight hikes in NSW?</h2>
  <p>
    NPWS runs a single online booking system that covers almost every campground
    in the state, including free bush campsites. You choose the campground and
    date, and pay any camping fee at the time of booking. Park entry fees are
    usually separate and charged per vehicle per day at parks with a vehicle
    gate.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your route runs through remote country with no bookable campground, or
    you plan to camp somewhere without facilities, NPWS asks hikers to lodge a
    free <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/safety/alpine-safety/kosciuszko-back-country-camping"
      rel="noopener">Trip Intention Form</a
    > before you set out. It is not a booking or a permit; it is a record of your
    planned route and return date so search and rescue have somewhere to start if
    you do not check back in.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Even a free NSW campsite usually needs an online booking. Assume you need
    one until the park page tells you otherwise.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What fire rules apply to multi day hikes in NSW?</h2>
  <p>
    NSW has a statewide <a
      href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/BFDP"
      rel="noopener">Bush Fire Danger Period</a
    > that runs from 1 October to 31 March each year, though NSW RFS can adjust it
    regionally. During this period, lighting a fire in the open generally needs a
    fire permit, though a small cooking or warmth fire on 2 metres of cleared ground
    is exempt from the permit requirement.
  </p>
  <p>
    Total Fire Bans are declared separately, on short notice, when conditions
    are dangerous. On a Total Fire Ban day the rules tighten further: only
    specific gas and electric appliances are exempt under the <a
      href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans/schedule-of-standard-exemptions-to-total-fire-bans"
      rel="noopener">Schedule of Standard Exemptions</a
    >, and solid fuel has no exemption at all. Check the <a
      href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans"
      rel="noopener">NSW RFS fire danger ratings and Total Fire Ban page</a
    > for your route before every trip, not just once at the start of the season,
    since bans can be declared with little warning during dry spells.
  </p>
  <p>
    A canister stove is the simplest way to stay compliant, since most models
    fall under the standard cooking exemptions on non-ban days, but you should
    still confirm current rules for your specific park and dates rather than
    assume last year's settings still apply.
  </p>

  <h2>What gear do you actually need for a multi day hike in NSW?</h2>
  <p>
    Route research matters more than gear for a first overnight hike, but the
    gear still has to work. Keep the list simple: a pack sized to the trip
    length, a tent or hammock rated for the conditions, a sleeping system
    matched to overnight temperatures, and a stove that suits the fire rules
    above.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Check your <a href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
        >sleeping bag temperature rating</a
      > against the coldest night on your route, not the daytime forecast.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pair it with enough <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/"
        >sleeping mat R-value</a
      > to stop the ground from cancelling out your bag's warmth.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <a href="/guides/2-person-hiking-tent-guide/">2-person hiking tent</a> gives
      most solo hikers welcome extra room for gear on multi-night trips.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run through the full <a href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/"
        >overnight hike packing list</a
      > before a longer route like Yuraygir or Green Gully.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are assembling a first kit from scratch, our <a href="/#kits"
      >beginner hiking kits</a
    > bundle a matched sleeping system and pack, and <a href="/build-my-kit/"
      >Build My Kit</a
    > fills in whatever you are missing without duplicating gear you already own.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Start with a short, well-serviced route like the Coast Track if this is your
    first overnight hike, then move up to a longer trip like Yuraygir or Green
    Gully once you have one trip behind you. Book your campsite online before
    you leave, lodge a Trip Intention Form for anything remote, and check NSW
    RFS fire rules for your dates every time, not just at the start of the
    season.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>What are the best overnight hikes in NSW for beginners?</summary>
      <p>
        The Coast Track in Royal National Park is a common starting point: a
        verified 27km, two-day walk with one bookable campsite at Garie Beach
        and train access at both ends. See the <a
          href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/things-to-do/walking-tracks/the-coast-track"
          rel="noopener">official NPWS page</a
        > for current bookings and fees.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Do you need to book campsites for multi day hikes in NSW?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Almost always, yes. NPWS books nearly every campground in the state
        online, including free ones. For remote camping with no bookable
        campground, lodge a Trip Intention Form instead.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >What is the best multi day hike in NSW outside the Blue Mountains?</summary
      >
      <p>
        It depends on your experience level. The Coast Track suits a first
        overnight trip, Yuraygir Coastal Walk and The Green Gully Track suit
        hikers ready for four to five days, and the Great North Walk suits
        anyone planning to section-hike a long-distance route over several
        trips.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>When is the NSW fire danger period?</summary>
      <p>
        The statutory Bush Fire Danger Period runs statewide from 1 October to
        31 March, though NSW RFS can vary it by region. Always check the current <a
          href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/BFDP"
          rel="noopener">NSW RFS Bush Fire Danger Period page</a
        > for your dates and location.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can you use a gas stove during a total fire ban in NSW?</summary>
      <p>
        Some gas and electric appliances are exempt under the NSW RFS Schedule
        of Standard Exemptions, but the conditions are specific and solid fuel
        has no exemption. Check the <a
          href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans/schedule-of-standard-exemptions-to-total-fire-bans"
          rel="noopener">current schedule</a
        > before you rely on any stove during a Total Fire Ban.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How long is the Great North Walk?</summary>
      <p>
        About 260km one-way between Sydney and Newcastle, split into ten stages
        of 10-20km so most hikers walk it as multiple shorter trips rather than
        in one continuous trek. See the <a
          href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/get-inspired/stories/why-this-260-km-hike-is-actually-not-that-hard"
          rel="noopener">NPWS overview</a
        > for section options.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is a Trip Intention Form the same as a campsite booking?</summary
      >
      <p>
        No. A Trip Intention Form records your planned route and return date for
        search and rescue purposes. It does not book or pay for a campsite, and
        you may still need a separate booking for any bookable campground on
        your route.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
